"use strict";(self["webpackChunkvue_blogdemo"]=self["webpackChunkvue_blogdemo"]||[]).push([[746],{8746:function(e,a,i){i.r(a),i.d(a,{default:function(){return d}});var t=i(641);const s={class:"blog-post"},n={class:"toc","aria-label":"On this page"};function o(e,a,i,o,r,l){const c=(0,t.g2)("router-link");return(0,t.uX)(),(0,t.CE)("div",s,[a[5]||(a[5]=(0,t.Lk)("h1",{id:"blog2-head"},"Sexuality Study",-1)),a[6]||(a[6]=(0,t.Lk)("p",{class:"article-meta"},"Course notes · WGST 111 · Spring 2026",-1)),(0,t.Lk)("nav",n,[(0,t.bF)(c,{to:{hash:"#s1"}},{default:(0,t.k6)(()=>a[0]||(a[0]=[(0,t.eW)("#1 To begin with",-1)])),_:1,__:[0]}),(0,t.bF)(c,{to:{hash:"#s2"}},{default:(0,t.k6)(()=>a[1]||(a[1]=[(0,t.eW)("#2 What is sexuality?",-1)])),_:1,__:[1]}),(0,t.bF)(c,{to:{hash:"#s3"}},{default:(0,t.k6)(()=>a[2]||(a[2]=[(0,t.eW)("#3 Intersectionality",-1)])),_:1,__:[2]}),(0,t.bF)(c,{to:{hash:"#s4"}},{default:(0,t.k6)(()=>a[3]||(a[3]=[(0,t.eW)("#4 Sex & gender",-1)])),_:1,__:[3]})]),a[7]||(a[7]=(0,t.Fv)('<div class="post-content"><section id="s1"><h2>#1 To begin with</h2><div class="blog"><p>During my exchange at UNC, I started to explore and document my studies on sexuality, a field of inquiry spanning psychology, sociology, biology, and personal identity. </p><p class="blog-ending">- a random day at the start of Spring 2026</p></div></section><section id="s2"><h2>#2 What is sexuality?</h2><div class="blog"><p>When it comes to sexuality, the first question is: What is it about? It can be about sexual identities, sexual desires, sexual behaviors... However, all these things appear to be private matters for individuals. There seems to be an invisible force that makes sexuality seem asocial, static, and unchangeable.</p><p> To demystify sexuality, we have to understand the social construction of sexuality, which can be examined through three contemporary approaches: queer theory, intersectionality, and political economy.</p><p> Actually, I was quite confused when I first heard about social construction. It seemed unrelated to sexuality, more like a term from sociology. However, socially constructed does not mean imaginary: these constructions are real and can change.</p><p> Sexuality is socially constructed, which means it changes with its social context. Looking at ideas about the purpose of sex, we can trace one historical perspective back to Puritanism, which treated sex outside heterosexual marriage as sinful and emphasized reproduction. During industrialization, ideas about sex and love changed alongside attitudes toward children and family life. In the nineteenth century, Victorian ideas associated love with femininity and sex with masculinity, distinctions that remain influential today. In the 1920s, ideas about sex and pleasure gained visibility alongside an economic boom. Entertainment such as nickelodeons and burlesque shows became popular, and socializing across gender became more common. Later, ideas of sexual egalitarianism and freedom gained prominence during the sexual revolution and second-wave feminism of the 1960s.</p><p> This brief history shows how understandings of sexuality evolve in economic, historical, and political contexts. Sexuality is a socially constituted historical practice. Other aspects of how people understand reality—including gender, race, and concepts such as family—are also shaped through shared understandings, language, history, and power relations.</p><p class="blog-ending">- 2026/03/05</p></div></section><section id="s3"><h2>#3 Intersectionality</h2><div class="blog"><p>How many words would you need to fully describe yourself? It may be hard to answer, but there are many ways to talk about how you experience the world and identify yourself: your gender, race, citizenship, native language, work, and where you live, among others. Sociologists recognize these as social identities, and they can carry substantial personal significance. Our cultures, societies, and interactions give them meaning. They can also shape our experiences of privilege and oppression.</p><p>In 1976, Emma DeGraffenreid and several other Black women sued General Motors for discrimination. They argued that the company segregated its workforce by race and gender: jobs available to Black workers were given to men, while jobs available to women were given to white women. The court dismissed their claims, refusing to let them combine race and gender into a single claim. As a result, their experiences fell through the cracks. This case helps illustrate intersectionality: race and gender can interact to shape experiences of discrimination that cannot be fully understood by examining either in isolation (Crenshaw, 2015).</p><p>Intersectionality is a way of thinking about identity and its relationship to power. It can also help us understand the complexity of sexuality. Sexuality does not exist in isolation; it intersects with other aspects of identity. Together, these intersections shape our experiences of privilege, oppression, and power.</p><div class="references"><h3>Reference</h3><p>Crenshaw, K. (2015, September 24). <a href="https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/in-theory/wp/2015/09/24/why-intersectionality-cant-wait/">Why intersectionality can’t wait</a>. <em>The Washington Post</em>.</p></div><p class="blog-ending">- 2026/03/06</p></div></section><section id="s4"><h2>#4 Sex &amp; gender</h2><div class="blog"><figure class="article-figure"><img src="/img/other/louis%20XIV.png" alt="A portrait of Louis XIV wearing high-heeled shoes"><figcaption>Louis XIV wearing high-heeled shoes.</figcaption></figure><p>Look at this portrait of Louis XIV wearing high heels. It may seem surprising that high heels worn by men once represented power and status, while today they are often associated with formal dress for women. Imagine a man wearing heels to show off his power today! This contrast suggests how people align themselves with groups and distinguish themselves from others. This is not always a bad thing: belonging to a group can bring us a sense of connection and joy. But differentiation can also become a form of classification through which power and privilege are distributed unevenly.</p><p>Sex is often used as a way to categorize people based on biological characteristics. We are born with primary sex characteristics, and secondary sex characteristics develop as we grow up. Sex is commonly treated as binary, although biological variation does not always fit neatly into two categories. Gender, on the other hand, concerns social meanings, including the ideas of masculinity and femininity that we attach to bodies. Fausto-Sterling’s work examines the relationship between sex and gender. Many beliefs about men and women do not simply arise from differences between male and female bodies; they are also produced and maintained through active, interactive, and cooperative social processes.</p><p>The common assumption that male-bodied people are masculine and female-bodied people are feminine treats gender as if it follows directly from sex. This assumption is part of an ideology often called the gender binary, but it does not capture everyone’s experience. In parts of early modern Europe, some scholars described male and female bodies through a one-sex model; Renaissance thinkers, for example, described female genitalia as an internal version of male genitalia. Biological differences do not make bodies opposites: we are not all the same, but we are not simply opposites either.</p><p>Nevertheless, most of us encounter two gender categories in everyday life, and binary thinking remains influential. We hear phrases such as “real men don’t cry” and “women are emotional.” Categories such as “men” and “women” can still say something meaningful about a person. But do any of us fit perfectly into a binary? We often speak and act as if gender stereotypes are true, even when they do not fully describe us. How much of what we take for granted is socially constructed?</p><p class="blog-ending">- 2026/11/02</p></div></section></div>',1)),(0,t.bF)(c,{to:"/blog",class:"back-link"},{default:(0,t.k6)(()=>a[4]||(a[4]=[(0,t.eW)("← Back to Writing",-1)])),_:1,__:[4]})])}var r={name:"SexualityStudy"},l=i(6262);const c=(0,l.A)(r,[["render",o]]);var d=c}}]);
